														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4784669">
									

<p>As a fellow resident of the Great Lakes region, I appreciate the care and respect that you took with this article. I'll always support a greater involvement and understanding of the culture and history of indigenous Americans &#8212; and a recognition that the land we live on has a human history much older than a few centuries.</p>
<p>That said, I wanted <em>more</em> from this. It never surprised me or introduced any wrinkles into the story, and I kept waiting for something truly interesting to happen. As is, it's just a series of Foundation agents continuing to poke the &quot;monster&quot; and failing laughably every time. If they're failing so badly, why do they keep trying the same way?</p>
I understand that's your point, but it kind of reads like you made the Foundation purposely incompetent just so you could get that point across. Couple that with a few
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">stylistic&nbsp;choices,</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">stylistic&nbsp;choices,</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>I love the first line of conprocs, but it really should include a few more lines about 1. The Foundation monitoring the area or 2. The Foundation preventing other groups from interfering. I love that The Foundation accepts they're not in charge of this thing, but c'mon. They're a shadowy governmental paranormal containment organization. They're not gonna <strong>completely ignore it</strong>.</li>
<li>Also, &quot;everything is solved instantly&quot; was unsatisfying as an ending. I would've maybe liked a bit harder-won of a conclusion, where The Foundation has to really grapple with their shortcomings rather than just say &quot;oh, you're right, thanks we'll fix that.&quot;</li>
<li>Finally, from your commentary it sounds like you aren't First Nations, so I respect that you wrote what you knew in terms of only taking the Foundation perspective. However, I think this piece suffered because the indigenous people depicted in it weren't really given characterizations: they were just presented as a monolithic group who dispensed info for The Foundation to misinterpret. Giving them names, dialogue, or more agency in the story could help that.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
and this one ultimately didn't work for me. I deeply, deeply respect your point, but I think the article's weaknesses ultimately push it into <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noble_savage">Noble Savage territory</a> as a work of fiction.
							</div>

								
