					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harryblank" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6479803); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6479803&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608231636" alt="HarryBlank" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6479803)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harryblank" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6479803); return false;" >HarryBlank</a></span> <span class="odate time_1601416498 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Sep 2020 21:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4784782">
									

<p>Some interesting points. As this story is set almost eighty years ago, far from the centre of Foundation activities, where there's no established Site and they're just muddling around in an emergency situation, I consider their ineptitude acceptable. Their cultural chauvinism, well, I made them <em>less</em> idiotic about that than their contemporaries would have been, because they <em>are</em> the Foundation. To the charge of subordinating a strict reading of the setting to the point the story was trying to make, I cheerfully plead intentional guilt. I'll do that every time.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Long&nbsp;and&nbsp;not&nbsp;entirely&nbsp;calm&nbsp;response&nbsp;follows!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Ugh</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I think the noble savage comparison is entirely off-base. The indigenous peoples are not presented as inherently nature-attuned primitives, or the morally pure, uncivilized best-in-all-of-us, but people who know their own cultural content and have ownership of it. That's all. They're a constellation of cultures who own and know their stories, and are confronted with people who don't. The fact that these stories are actually <em>true</em> in the context of the tale removes any romanticization of their religious beliefs, in my opinion. You're not a picaresque, quaint society if you're in consultation with big corporations and describing actual events in scientifically-correct terms.</p>
<p>There's no reference to their living conditions or &quot;civilization&quot; save for the lack of clean drinking water, which has nothing to do with them directly. They act with agency to mobilize culture to acquire modern conveniences, as they are <em>not</em> savages but members of a modern society. They know what they do want, and what they don't want, from their neighbours. Where there are references to their cultural beliefs in relation to nature or myth, those are gleaned directly from indigenous sources - and those beliefs are being relayed by people whose job in society is to relay them, in real life, even today. I'm not applying tropes to the First Peoples, or presenting them as signifiers rather than human beings, I'm repeating their words and cultural understandings in their own terms. I was in fact very careful to never describe their belief systems in terms that are my own. There is none of me in that depiction. Not an ounce. I have taken liberties only with the non-indigenous characters.</p>
<p>Not every story about natives who aren't wrong is the noble savage trope, which I am very well aware of. I tend towards death of the author in most cases, but I firmly believe that's a misreading - and I resent it, as it's a misreading with considerable moral freight. It seems like a reflexive mislabeling to me, one with a chilling effect. That's not a term I'd throw around without making sure it applied.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

