<p>Thanks for your response.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;collapsed&nbsp;reply</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;collapsed&nbsp;reply</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I accept your point about the Foundation's competency compared to others of the time, and while I still didn't like its execution, the reasoning still helps me to justify it more now.</p>
<p>I wasn't saying the story actually paints the Anishnaabe as &quot;noble savages,&quot; and you're right that simply having native people be correct doesn't automatically put the story in that camp. My comparison to the trope was more because, at least to me, the story didn't have any native voices.</p>
<p>Because the article's structure is so straightforward (Foundation Gets Advice, They Misapply It, Things Go Wrong, repeat), we don't get a chance to encounter the Anishnaabe or any of their members as <em>characters</em>. They're just presented as a source of information about the entity, not active participants in the narrative. I'm not saying they don't exercise agency in their cultural/economic situation &#8212; I'm saying they don't exercise agency <em>in the story</em>.</p>
<p>Incorporating an active native voice into this article would have been difficult though, and would have significantly changed its structure, so I understand why you didn't do it. I still think it would've been nice to have a character from one of the native peoples become actively involved in the storyline &#8212; because without that, it's a fairly cut-and-dry morality play with no twists or complications. The Foundation was in the wrong, they fix it, end of story.</p>
<p>I wasn't trying to accuse you of any ill intent or judgment on the cultures that feature in your story. It's abundantly clear that you have deep respect for them, and that you put time and effort into treating their involvement with that same respect and accuracy. I just wanted to share why it didn't work for me personally, and make a reference to what it reminded me of. I apologize for any perceived slight, as that was never my intention.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

