														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4794446">
									

<p>I appreciate the response, and I don't disagree. Ideally, I would have co-authored this with an indi&#8230; no, actually, <em>ideally,</em> an indigenous author would've written this themselves. I had my point I wanted to make, though, and I tried to make it as respectfully as I could. That did result in a certain flatness in the character arcs, as I was unwilling to put words in their mouths or depict them taking action, which would have felt too much like puppeteering. This wouldn't have been a problem if someone from the respective cultures had been involved&#8230; or if someone less worried about misrepresentation had been writing.</p>
<p>I don't know that they lack agency in comparison to the Foundation characters, as it's their desires that end up becoming the <em>status quo,</em> but that's a matter of perspective and I agree that there's room for them to have taken a more active role in the story. If I'd had the balls to find an Anishnaabe coauthor, that might have happened; something to consider down the line.</p>
<p>Thanks for engaging in this conversation with me.</p>

