				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/t-rutherford" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4620432); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4620432&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608231636" alt="T Rutherford" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4620432)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/t-rutherford" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4620432); return false;" >T Rutherford</a></span> <span class="odate time_1601561240 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Oct 2020 14:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4786172">
									

<p>Well done. Easy +1, but like most people I have my gripes.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">PROS</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">PROS</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>As an Ontario resident, it was great seeing some representation for the Great Lakes and the mythology surrounding them. You clearly put the time in on this, both in terms of research, extrapolation, and respect for the source material.</li>
<li>There's a lot of strong, technical writing here without burdening the reader with too much terminology. This also might be the first time in recent history I've seen an article using CSR style endnotes, rather than footnotes directly after a clause (i.e. the Chimera description). It felt very professional, clinical, and even bureaucratic. That's not an easy tone to keep while describing the anomalous, let alone as engagingly as you do.</li>
<li>The story itself has a good, well paced arc that shows a gradual decline in the Foundations hubris. The real-life references to both Great Lakes mythology and modern issues in Canada (pipeline blockades, destructive mining practices, etc.) help ground the article extremely well.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">CONS</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">CONS</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>While the technical writing is strong, the formatting feels a bit flat. The experiment logs are functional, but lack punch, and tend to linger a bit too long with the &quot;theory&quot; portions. I feel you could have folded theory and procedure together without too much trimming; you tend to repeat information in 'what we should do' and then 'what we did' to the articles detriment.</li>
<li>The interstitial story-beats between the experiment logs also feel a bit ill-paced. They're either too short, too long, or provide critical information that should have been introduced in the description <em>before</em> the experiments. Your way reads far better in terms of pacing, but not in terms of clinical formatting. Trust me, I'm super guilty of this too.</li>
<li>You tend to go a bit overboard on the footnotes - which sucks - because I'd say half the footnotes with necessary information / story building are just great. We don't need to hear so much about what's illegal, and information that's unfounded can be noted in the text proper.</li>
<li>In retrospect, while I love how grounded this story is, it also feels like you didn't break much ground, so to speak. It's all very rooted in mythology without proposing any new or interesting twists on the source material. Yes, they exist. Yes, we shouldn't be destroying their environment. Yes, let's just leave them alone. It's well written, but a tad dry without more of a twist.</li>
<li>The ending is great, in theory. You sum up the main point of the article well with the &quot;it's not our lake&quot;, but it feels odd coming from a journal entry. Like, what Foundation Archivist would put that quote at the end of the article? An official statement from the Site or Research director, carrying the same sentiment, might land better. This feels a bit too, I don't know, <em>sassy</em> for the closing of such a clinical document.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, fantastic work. I look forward to your future articles!</p>

							</div>

								
